1. Definitions

"Comik" means the software and documentation in this repository, in source or built form.

"Derivative" means any fork, clone, port, modification or other work that contains or is based on a
substantial part of Comik, including a rebuilt or rebranded copy.

"Ads" means advertising of any kind: display, banner, video or native ads, sponsored or promoted content,
affiliate or referral links added for revenue, paid placements, and advertising or ad-tracking SDKs, whether
shown inside an app, on a website, or on a hosted service.

2. What you may do

Subject to sections 3 to 5, you may:

a. read and study the source code;
b. run Comik for any purpose, personal or commercial;
c. copy, modify and distribute Comik and your Derivatives, in source or built form;
d. contribute changes back to Comik, as described in section 6.

3. Attribution (required)

Every copy and every Derivative of Comik that you distribute or host must:

a. keep this license file and every copyright notice that is in the code;
b. state clearly, where a person using it can see it, that it is based on Comik by Suryansh Kapil, with a
   link to https://github.com/asuka-405/comik. In an app or website, that means its About page, settings
   screen or footer. In a repository, it means the README. A hidden source comment does not satisfy this;
c. say that you changed it, if you did, and not present your changes as the Licensor's;
d. not suggest that the Licensor endorses, sponsors or is responsible for your Derivative, and not use the
   name "Comik" or its logo as the name of a different product in a way that could confuse people about who
   makes it.

4. Advertising (permission required)

You may not show, serve or include Ads in or with Comik or any Derivative, and may not distribute or host a
copy of Comik or a Derivative that does, unless the Licensor has given you permission in writing first.
The Licensor may refuse, or may grant permission with conditions, such as revenue sharing or a notice to
users. A permission applies only to the person and the project it names. Contact the Licensor through the
repository's issue tracker or the address on the Licensor's GitHub profile.

